  "body": "Conjecture: open:creib.forge.conformance.oracle.recover_json_object. The documented rule says, \"The object scored is the last one inside a code fence when any fence holds one, else the last top-level object in the text.\" Reply A is '```json\\n{\"x\": 1} prose\\n```\\n{\"y\": 2}'. Reply B is '```json\\n{\"z\": 3} prose\\n```\\n{\"y\": 2}'. For A, the fence holds the top-level object {\"x\": 1}, so the rule requires recover_json_object(A) to return {\"x\": 1}. For B, the fence holds {\"z\": 3}, so the rule requires recover_json_object(B) to return {\"z\": 3}. The check treats the entire fenced content as a single candidate; both fenced contents fail to parse, so it falls through to the top-level pool and returns the last top-level object {\"y\": 2} for both replies. Thus the check gives the same answer on a pair the rule requires it to tell apart. I would be wrong if recover_json_object(A) returns {\"x\": 1} or recover_json_object(B) returns {\"z\": 3}, or if the two answers differ.",
